# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The door
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everyone who reads the drive types this same word. Any email address works
# alongside it — the address is recorded, never checked, and a new one simply
# becomes a new account.
#
# Be clear-eyed about a four-digit default: it is 10,000 guesses. What makes
# that survivable is the rate limit in lib/auth/rate-limit, which counts every
# attempt per IP in Postgres — not in memory, because an in-process counter on
# a serverless host means ten attempts per instance rather than ten per
# address. Set something longer than this on anything that matters.
#
# A BLANK value falls back to the default below. An empty string is not
# nullish, so `?? "1234"` would leave it "" and the door would then refuse
# every reader for every word typed, with no error and no log. See the long
# note in lib/auth/config.
SITE_PASSWORD=1234

# The administrator's word. NO DEFAULT, deliberately: unset means nobody can
# administer, which is the safe direction for this one.
#
# NEVER make this SITE_PASSWORD with characters added. Two passwords where one
# is a prefix of the other survive `===` and are broken by the first well-meant
# "be forgiving about trailing whitespace" edit anyone makes near them. Pick an
# unrelated word.
ADMIN_PASSWORD=1234

# The addresses that may upload, rename and delete — comma, semicolon or
# whitespace separated. BOTH halves are required: the address must be on this
# list AND the admin word must be typed. Neither alone is enough, and an
# address here that types the reader word is an ordinary reader for that
# session.
#
# Read from the environment on every request rather than stamped into the
# cookie, so removing an address takes effect on the next request rather than
# whenever an eight-hour session happens to expire.
#
# Left blank, NOBODY is an administrator. That is deliberate: an unset variable
# must not match a blank address and hand the drive to the next visitor.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# Session cookie signing key. Any long random string:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it signs everyone out. REQUIRED in production — unset, sign-in is
# disabled outright rather than signing cookies with something guessable.
AUTH_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The project URL. Also read at BUILD time by next.config.ts, which puts this
# origin in the CSP's connect-src — the browser PUTs upload bytes straight to
# Storage, because Vercel's free tier will not carry a 20MB request body.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# The service-role key. It bypasses row-level security completely: it is the
# database with no restrictions.
#
# NEVER prefix this NEXT_PUBLIC_. Next inlines anything with that prefix into
# the client bundle, by name, without asking. lib/supabase/admin.ts imports
# "server-only" so that a client component reaching for it is a build error
# rather than a silent catastrophe.
#
# There is no anon key anywhere in this project and none is needed: the browser
# talks to Supabase exactly once, through a signed upload URL that carries its
# own scoped token.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cron
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vercel sends this as `Authorization: Bearer <value>` to /api/cron/maintain.
# Checked rather than trusted-by-obscurity, because that endpoint deletes rows:
# unguarded it is a URL anyone can call to run the reaper whenever they like.
# With no value set the route refuses every request rather than running openly.
#
# The cron does three things daily: touch `heartbeat` so the free Supabase
# project never hits its seven-day inactivity pause, reap abandoned uploads,
# and retry storage deletions that failed. Vercel's Hobby plan allows one run
# per day, production only.
CRON_SECRET=
